      "closure_rationale": "Dynamic guard authority is separate from const qualification and pointer lifetime. An original single-threaded raw-alias demonstration changes a cell through its exposed representation while a reader guard remains live; this is defined C access but an intentionally bypassed API contract, not a native data race. Safe-language RefCell guard lifetimes remain distinct from C caller discipline. The adapter actually imports ow_borrow/release and ow_cell_read/write/replace. One pthread mutex protects both permission registry and value. Callback updates retain a real writer while invoking user code outside that mutex; reentrant acquisition, replacement and nested update return BUSY. Candidate rejection and selected close preserve caller output and value while retaining callback side effects. Closing stops new work but existing guards drain, and final destruction requires externally joined/quiescent callers. Six C17 profiles execute seven anchors, six compiled controls and seven compiling-stub rejections. Boundaries cover shared/readonly/empty/stale guards, output canaries, every24four-reader release permutation, cross-cell identity and final serial release. An independent56-case table checks callback admission/failure/close outcomes. Actual OW_MUTANT5/15 retain replacement and reader-write regressions; new controls isolate callback permission/publication defects. The required multiple-thread variant is implemented with actual pthread synchronization around the entire guard/value protocol. Four workers complete4000updates; a separately paused worker callback is closed, resumed and joined before destruction, preserving staged outputs. Both GCC and Clang separately execute the actual thread and paused-close cases under C17 TSan without diagnostics. These observed histories do not prove arbitrary interleavings, fairness or recovery from unsupported cancellation/nonlocal exits. Independent delayed learner performance remains unassessed. Bounded author support only."
    },
